WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.150
Start and controls
Take a seat and press Ready. The host starts when everyone is ready. Your cards or dice are at the bottom.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:17.450
Follow the turn prompt. Wait for animations to finish; an expired turn is played automatically. Use Return to room after settlement.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:25.449
Chess: an 8×8 board
We keep white’s perspective throughout: our side is at the bottom and moves first, with black at the top. Every piece is public and there is no handicap.

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:35.449
Goal: checkmate the king
An attacked king is in check; if the check cannot be answered, it is checkmate and that player loses. Winning material is not itself a win.

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:43.149
Select a piece, then a legal square
Select your piece to see its legal squares, then choose one. Mouse moves immediately; touch previews before Confirm or a second tap on the destination.

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:49.449
The move list uses standard algebraic notation: Nc3 means a knight moves to c3. You can resign at any time, or offer a draw on your turn if the opponent agrees.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:01:01.549
The six pieces | Repeated moves accelerated
We now speed up: knights develop, pawns advance and capture, and a queen comes out. Selecting a piece shows only squares legal in the current position.

00:01:01.549 --> 00:01:11.448
The king moves one square; the queen any distance in a straight or diagonal line; rooks straight and bishops diagonally, neither jumping; knights move in an L and may jump; pawns advance one square (two from the start) and capture diagonally forward.

00:01:11.448 --> 00:01:20.248
In check: answer it immediately
The black queen reaches e7 and attacks our king along the e-file. After a check the next move must remove it, and the board offers only legal replies; we block with a bishop.

00:01:20.248 --> 00:01:27.448
There are three answers: move the king, capture the attacker, or block the line. No move may leave your own king in check.

00:01:27.448 --> 00:01:37.348
Another answer: capture the attacker
A black knight goes to b4. After our pawn move it captures the pawn on c2 with check, and our queen captures the knight.

00:01:37.348 --> 00:01:45.447
A knight’s attack cannot be blocked, so move the king or capture it. The king also cannot step onto an attacked square.

00:01:45.447 --> 00:02:03.447
Fast-forward through the middlegame
We speed up: our bishop gives check and the blocking pawn is captured; our pawn captures again, opening the bishop’s line and forcing the black king aside.

00:02:03.447 --> 00:02:13.347
A pawn on the last rank: choose a piece
The white pawn captures the rook on a8 and must promote. A picker opens; we choose the queen. The black bishop then recaptures it.

00:02:13.347 --> 00:02:21.447
A pawn may become a queen, rook, bishop or knight, but cannot remain a pawn. The new piece can be captured like any other.

00:02:21.447 --> 00:02:29.147
Castling
Select the king, then g1. The king moves two squares toward the rook and the rook jumps to its other side, tucking the king away and activating the rook.

00:02:29.147 --> 00:02:35.447
Neither the king nor that rook may have moved, and no piece may stand between them. The king may not castle out of, through or into check. Kingside is O-O; queenside is O-O-O.

00:02:35.447 --> 00:02:42.047
Rule card: en passant
Rule explanation; it does not occur in this game. If an enemy pawn advances two squares from its start and lands beside our pawn, our pawn may capture it by moving to the square it crossed.

00:02:42.047 --> 00:02:47.449
It must be done on the very next move or the chance is lost. Select the pawn, then the empty square, as for any pawn capture; it is written exd6.

00:02:47.449 --> 00:02:55.149
Rule card: automatic draws
Rule explanation. The server declares these immediately, with no claim needed: stalemate, the third occurrence of a position, 50 moves each without a capture or pawn move, and insufficient material.

00:02:55.149 --> 00:03:01.449
Stalemate means the side to move is not in check but has no legal move. Insufficient material covers only king versus king, king plus one bishop or knight versus king, and bishops of one square colour.

00:03:01.449 --> 00:03:12.449
Check after check until mate
Our knight steps aside and the queen checks along the c-file. The bishop blocks and is captured with check; the king returns to d8 and the queen goes to a8 with no escape.

00:03:12.449 --> 00:03:21.449
Checkmate is check with no way to move the king, capture the attacker or block the line.

00:03:21.449 --> 00:03:29.149
Result: white wins by checkmate
After 33 moves the black king cannot answer check, so our white side wins. The result lists the checkmate reason and move count; use Back to room.

00:03:29.149 --> 00:03:36.449
In a draw both players share first place. A timeout never loses; the system plays a legal move for you.
